  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM EDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nThe forecast area has begun the drying trend for the week, as rain \nmoved out of southern portions of the area last night. At the time \nof this discussion, a dry cold front is just to our west; skies are \nclearing just behind a line from Memphis to Nashville to \nMiddlesboro, KY. Skies will clear for us today from approximately NW \nto SE. By the later afternoon everyone should be experiencing mostly \nsunny skies.\n\nThe week ahead can be characterized by weak flow primarily out of \nthe N to W and surface high pressure moving from the Upper Plains to \noff of the east coast mid week. New surface high pressure will \nreplace it the latter part of the week as troughing and unsettled \nweather remain locked to the north and our south.\n\nTemperatures will remain above normal, even today following the \nfrontal passage. A gradual warming trend is expected with mid to \npossibly upper 80s from the central to southern valley to round out \nthe work week. The coolest morning of the week will be tomorrow \nmorning when the upper half of our forecast area if you were to \nslice it in half, will see lows in the 40s; 50s for lows for the \nremainder of the area.\n\nAlthough too far to say for certain with any specifics, our next \npossible chance of rain won't be until next weekend when a shortwave \nrides north within the subtropical jet. Underneath it at the \nsurface, a possible tropical system may need watching from the Gulf \nCoast's perspective. As of last night, the NHC gives the area in \nquestion a 40 percent chance of development. Something to watch \nfor us as it may bring another rain filled and dreary weekend.",
